  "rationale": "**Judgment:** `requirements.definitions.capture` is satisfied. The implementation covers each obligation and is marked `{impl}`. The tests cover the main points of the design space.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `add_input` (src/add_input.rs) sends every target, whether a requirement or an example, through `add_definition_inputs`. That function loads the existing record or creates a new one, and calls `update_inputs` with the subject's current text.\n- `update_inputs` first adds or refreshes the requested inputs. It then calls `terms::dependencies` (src/terms.rs). That function computes the transitive closure of term dependencies from three sources:\n  - terms referenced in the subject text;\n  - terms referenced in every reviewed input, whether a file range, Rust item, requirements glob, or an existing definition input;\n  - terms referenced in the bodies of the terms already collected, with cycles handled by a `required` set.\n- For each required definition, `update_inputs` resolves a definition input. If the record does not hold exactly one matching input, or that input differs from the resolved one, it removes all inputs for that definition, pushes a single fresh one, and marks the record changed. This covers \"capture or refresh\" and prevents duplicates. It also repairs a record that already contains duplicates.\n- When anything changed, `update_inputs` sets `determination` to false and replaces the rationale with a review-pending message.\n- `add_definition_inputs` writes the file only if `update_inputs` reports a change. Otherwise the record stays byte-identical, which meets \"If no inputs or other record contents change, the record MUST remain unchanged.\"\n- Both functions carry `{impl}` markers for this requirement. `terms::dependencies` carries `{impl}` for `requirements.definitions.dependencies` and `requirements.definitions.selection`. The other behavior in these functions is specified by `requirements.cli.add_input` and its sub-requirements. I found no unmarked code that implements part of this requirement. The authoring path (`definition_inputs`) is marked for its own requirements.\n\n**Tests** (tests/definitions.rs)\n- `cyclic_dependencies_are_captured_once_and_changes_require_review`:\n  - It captures from a glob over a fixture with cyclic terms and expects exactly 3 inputs.\n  - It approves the record, then re-captures and asserts the file is byte-identical.\n  - It changes the glossary (the phrase and a term body). Validation reports \"input text changed\", and the record is untouched until `capture` is re-run.\n  - After the re-run, `determination` is false and there are still 3 inputs.\n  - It deletes a definition input, which validation reports as \"found 0\". It duplicates one, which validation reports as \"found 2\". A further `capture` repairs the record to 3 inputs.\n- `example_subject_and_definition_display_use_the_same_dependencies`: an example subject's own term references are captured, giving 3 inputs, and the displayed definition matches.\n- `fixture`, `capture` and `approve` are helper functions marked `{test}`.\n- Other tests in the file cover captures from Rust-item inputs and globs. These tests are marked for the dependencies and selection requirements rather than this one.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps)**\n- Definition inputs that are no longer required are retained, because existing definition inputs are treated as roots. The requirement does not ask for their removal.\n- The record's stored subject text is not rewritten when the definition text changes. That belongs to the general `add_input` behavior, not to this requirement.",
